# What is the smallest number you can't describe in a few words? Berry's paradox
tags: ["berry paradox","kolmogorov complexity","chaitin","randomness","incompleteness"]
teaches: ["concepts/berry-paradox","concepts/kolmogorov-complexity","concepts/chaitin-incompleteness"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why does 'the smallest number not nameable in under sixty letters' name one in under sixty letters? How did Chaitin turn this into a proof that most truths about randomness can't be proved?"
next: {"title":"Can maths prove everything? Gödel's incompleteness theorems","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/06-can-maths-prove-everything-godel-s.cast"}

## Berry's paradox
sources:
    - id: russell1906
      title: Les paradoxes de la logique
      authors: Bertrand Russell
      year: 1906
      finding: First printed statement of the paradox of the least integer not nameable in few syllables, credited to G. G. Berry of the Bodleian Library (Revue de métaphysique et de morale 14).
    - id: russell1908
      title: Mathematical logic as based on the theory of types
      authors: Bertrand Russell
      year: 1908
      finding: 'Restates Berry''s paradox and traces it, with the others, to a vicious circle: whatever involves all of a collection must not be one of the collection (American Journal of Mathematics 30).'
    - id: kolmogorov1965
      title: Three approaches to the quantitative definition of information
      authors: A. N. Kolmogorov
      year: 1965
      finding: Defines the complexity of an object as the length of the shortest program that produces it (Problems of Information Transmission 1).
    - id: chaitin1966
      title: On the length of programs for computing finite binary sequences
      authors: Gregory J. Chaitin
      year: 1966
      finding: Independently defines program-size complexity; written while Chaitin was a student at City College, New York (Journal of the ACM 13).
    - id: chaitin1974
      title: Information-theoretic limitations of formal systems
      authors: Gregory J. Chaitin
      year: 1974
      finding: A formal system cannot prove that any specific string has complexity much greater than the system's own size, though almost all strings do (Journal of the ACM 21).

