    card title "The smallest number you can't describe"

A: Here's a puzzle that sounds like a parlour game: what is the smallest whole number you can't describe in a few words?
    draw h_num h_name h_let h_rule

A: Small numbe (@draw r1n r1s r1l@)rs are easy. Seven takes five le (@draw r2n r2s r2l@)tters; one million takes ten.

A: Big numbers ca (@draw r3n@)n be cheap too. One million, forty-eight thousand, five hundred and seve (@draw r3s@)nty-six is just 'two to the twentieth': s (@draw r3l@)eventeen letters.

    pause 0.3

A: So let's set a limit: a descri (@draw lim@)ption is short if it has fewer than sixty letters, not counting spaces.

A: Which, then, i (@draw r4n@)s the smallest number with no short description at all?

A: The answer is a contradiction, first printed i (@draw russell@)n 1906 by Bertrand Russell, who credited it to (@draw berry@) G. G. Berry, a junior librarian at Oxford's Bodleian Library.

    erase russell berry

A: Here's the route: an argume (@draw rt1@)nt where every step looks innocent and the end is impossible; then the  (@draw rt2@)broken step; then how Gregory Chaitin turned (@draw rt3@) it into a theorem about randomness.

    clear

    card title "Four innocent steps"

A: Step o (@draw s1@)ne. There are only so many ways to arrange fewer than sixty letters: an enormous number,  (@draw finite@)but a finite one.

A: Each phrase describes at most one number, so only finitely many numbers get a short description.
    highlight s1

A: Step (@draw a12 s2 inf@) two. The whole numbers never end, so most of them are left without one.

A: Step three (@draw a23 s3@). Any collection of whole numbers has a smallest member. Call the smallest left-out number B.

    erase finite inf

A: Step four. Now describe B (@draw ph@): the smallest number not nameable in under sixty letters.

    draw guess

    ask "How many letters does that phrase have? Spaces don't count. Click your guess." on guess store g default "35" voice a
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it's {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's {g.true}.

    erase guess

A: Forty-seven: three, eight, si (@draw a34 s4@)x, three, eight, two, five, five and seven.
    draw count

    erase count

A: So B has a short d (@draw clash@)escription after all. It can (@draw imp@)'t be left out and described at the same time.

    pause 0.5

A: Every step looked innocent. So which one is wrong?
    highlight s1 s2 s3 s4

    erase clash imp

    ask "Which step would most logicians blame? Tap it." choose ["s1","s2","s3","s4"] answer s4 store blame stage own voice a
        right: Step four: the phrase leans on 'nameable', a word about every phrase, itself included.
        wrong: Steps one to three are counting. Which step leans on a word about naming?

    highlight s4 duration 1.2

A: Step four. 'Nameable'  (@draw loop itself@)is a word about every phrase in English, this one included, so what the phrase names depends on what the phrase itself names.

    fade s4 to 0.35 duration 0.6

A: Russell's own rule was that whatever talks about a (@draw nothing@)ll of a collection can't itself be one of them; so the phrase names nothing.

A: Others say ordinary English is simply too loose to settle what 'nameable' means.
    highlight ph

    erase loop itself

A: Either way, the cure is the same: swap 'de (@draw cure@)scribed in English' for something perfectly precise.

    clear

    card title "Programs as names"

A: In the mid-1960s a New York tee (@draw chaitin@)nager, Gregory Chaitin, took that step, at about the same time  (@draw kolmo@)as Andrey Kolmogorov in Moscow.

    erase chaitin kolmo

A: A descr (@draw hp@)iption becomes a computer program (@draw hpa hs@) that prints the thing, and its length is counted in bits, the zeros and ones it's written in.

A: Take twenty bits: zer (@draw sa@)o one, zero one, over and over. A tiny program  (@draw pa pa_n@)prints it: print zero one, ten times.

A: Now take twenty coin to (@draw sb@)sses. With no pattern, the shortest program i (@draw pb pb_n@)s just 'print', followed by the whole string.

A: That's what random means (@draw rand@) here: a string with no description shorter than itself.

A: And random strings aren't rare. They're nearly all of them.
    highlight sb rand

A: There are just over a million strings of twenty bits.
    draw tally_box tally_line_1

A: But programs shorter than ten bits number o (@draw tally_line_2@)ne, plus two, plus four, and so on up to 512: just 1,023 in all.

A: So fewer than one stri (@draw tally_line_3@)ng in a thousand can be squeezed to half its length.

A: It's why no zip pr (@draw zip@)ogram can shrink every file: there aren't enough short files to go round.

A: So nearly every statement 'this string is random' is true. Can we prove them?
    highlight sb rand

    clear

    card title "A Berry phrase that runs"

A: Take any rule (@draw p_rule@)book for mathematics, its axioms and proof rules. Say, for illustration, it takes  (@draw z_rule@)ten thousand bits to write down.

A: Now write Berry's phrase as a pr (@draw frame frame_l p_search@)ogram: go through every proof the rulebook allows, in order, and print the first st (@draw out_a out@)ring  (@draw p_mil@)proved to need more than a million bits.

A: How long is that program? Ten thousand bits of rulebook, say three  (@draw z_search@)hundred for the search, and twenty fo (@draw z_mil@)r the number o (@draw z_tot@)ne million, written in binary.

A: So a program of about ten thousand bits would print a string proved to need over a million.
    blocking false

    highlight z_tot duration 2.5

    highlight out duration 2.5

A: That's Berry's contradiction again, but now every word is precise, so something real has to give.
    highlight out

A: If the rulebook proves only true  (@draw x_out@)things, the search neve (@draw never@)r finds such a proof. It runs forever.

    erase out_a out x_out never

A: A million was arbitr (@draw bar_axis bar_lab bar_prov bar_prov_l@)ary: any bound well above the rulebook's size fails the same way. That's Chaitin's theorem: a rulebook can't prove that any on (@draw bar_tick@)e string is more complex than about its own size.

A: Yet nearly eve (@draw bar_true bar_true_l@)ry long string is far more complex than that. True statements about r (@highlight bar_true@)andomness are everywhere, and almost none can be proved.

    pause 0.5

A: So the broken step was the word 'nameable'. Pin (@draw fix@) it down as 'printed by a short program', and the contradiction tu (@highlight bar_prov bar_true@)rns into a limit on proof, a close cousin of Gödel's incompleteness theorem.

    quiz "A rulebook one megabyte long proves that a certain string needs a program over a gigabyte. What follows?" voice a
        right: The rulebook proves falsehoods: its search program, about a megabyte, would print that string, so the proof must be false.
        wrong: Think of the search program built from that rulebook: how long is it, and what would it print?
        * Likely: most are random
        + The rulebook proves lies
        * Fine: long proofs work

    clear

    card title "The smallest number you can't describe"

A: (@draw h_num h_name h_let h_rule r1n r1s r1l r2n r2s r2l r3n r3s r3l r4n@) So, the smallest number you can't describe in a few words? In English, th (@draw end_ph end47@)e question bites its own tail. With programs, it has an answer, and no rulebook ca (@highlight r4n@)n prove which number it is.

## Where next
end_page: true

