    '|a||Now write Berry''s phrase as a program: go through every proof the rulebook allows, in order, and print the first string proved to need more than a million bits.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1qorjfy'
    '|a||How long is that program? Ten thousand bits of rulebook, say three hundred for the search, and twenty for the number one million, written in binary.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:ge8ihg'
    '|a||So a program of about ten thousand bits would print a string proved to need over a million.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6720
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:13hh2z0'
    '|a||That''s Berry''s contradiction again, but now every word is precise, so something real has to give.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:xj0t2q'
    '|a||If the rulebook proves only true things, the search never finds such a proof. It runs forever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1lyu7x'
    '|a||A million was arbitrary: any bound well above the rulebook''s size fails the same way. That''s Chaitin''s theorem: a rulebook can''t prove that any one string is more complex than about its own size.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:x8n3dc'
    '|a||Yet nearly every long string is far more complex than that. True statements about randomness are everywhere, and almost none can be proved.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1mcyrz1'
    '|a||So the broken step was the word ''nameable''. Pin it down as ''printed by a short program'', and the contradiction turns into a limit on proof, a close cousin of Gödel''s incompleteness theorem.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:e5ry2c'
    '|a||A rulebook one megabyte long proves that a certain string needs a program over a gigabyte. What follows?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1rs0rbb'
    '|a||The rulebook proves falsehoods: its search program, about a megabyte, would print that string, so the proof must be false.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||So, the smallest number you can''t describe in a few words? In English, the question bites its own tail. With programs, it has an answer, and no rulebook can prove which number it is.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11600
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Next: Can maths prove everything? Gödel''s incompleteness theorems':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Steps one to three are counting. Which step leans on a word about naming?':
